{"acceptedResponses":["E"],"answer":"E","bank":"amc","batch":"B","choiceOrder":["A","B","C","D","E"],"choices":{"A":"\\(2\\) divides \\(n\\)","B":"\\(3\\) divides \\(n\\)","C":"\\(6\\) divides \\(n\\)","D":"\\(7\\) divides \\(n\\)","E":"\\(n\\gt84\\)"},"choicesType":"text","domain":[],"editorialNote":"","figureSources":{},"id":"2002_AMC_12B_Q04","knowledgeGraph":{"assessedIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations"],"contentHash":"6ce1b8c4eddae763f228c41306d555452c99fc461299a944dd1afa822c0b8ec7","flags":[],"reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-and-solve","label":"Bound the integer sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-and-solve":["cnm_p169","cnm_165"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p169","cnm_165"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"divisibility-constraints","label":"Use divisibility constraints","methodDirectNodeIds":{"divisibility-constraints":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_p153"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_p153"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p174","cnm_016","cnm_p153"],"id":"bound-integer-sum","label":"Bound the integer sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-integer-sum":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_p169"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_p169","cnm_p174","cnm_016","cnm_p153"]}],"status":"mapped","supportIds":["cnm_p174","cnm_p153"],"version":"1.0.0"},"level":"AMC 12","number":4,"question":"Let \\(n\\) be a positive integer such that \\(\\dfrac12+\\dfrac13+\\dfrac17+\\dfrac1n\\) is an integer. Which of the following statements is not true:","questionType":"text","rejectedAnswers":[],"responseType":"choice","schemaVersion":"3.0","skill":[],"solution":"First combine the fixed fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac17\n=\\frac{21+14+6}{42}\n=\\frac{41}{42}.\n\\]\nLet \\(S\\) be the full sum. Since \\(n\\) is a positive integer, \\(n\\ge1\\), so \\(0<1/n\\le1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{41}{42}<S\\le\\frac{83}{42}<2.\n\\]\nThe only integer in this range is \\(1\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\frac1n=1-\\frac{41}{42}=\\frac1{42},\n\\]\nso \\(n=42\\). This positive value does make the original sum equal to \\(1\\).\n\nThe number \\(42\\) is divisible by \\(2\\), \\(3\\), \\(6\\), and \\(7\\), but it is not greater than \\(84\\). Thus the statement that is not true is \\(\\boxed{n>84}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).","solutionAvailability":"present","solutionMethods":[{"directNodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_p169"],"explanation":"First combine the fixed fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac13+\\frac17\n=\\frac{21+14+6}{42}\n=\\frac{41}{42}.\n\\]\nLet \\(S\\) be the full sum. Since \\(n\\) is a positive integer, \\(n\\ge1\\), so \\(0<1/n\\le1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{41}{42}<S\\le\\frac{83}{42}<2.\n\\]\nThe only integer in this range is \\(1\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\frac1n=1-\\frac{41}{42}=\\frac1{42},\n\\]\nso \\(n=42\\). This positive value does make the original sum equal to \\(1\\).\n\nThe number \\(42\\) is divisible by \\(2\\), \\(3\\), \\(6\\), and \\(7\\), but it is not greater than \\(84\\). Thus the statement that is not true is \\(\\boxed{n>84}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).","id":"bound-integer-sum","label":"Bound the integer sum","legacyRouteId":"bound-integer-sum","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Accepted primary worked-solution review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evidence/solutions-2026-11-06/2002_AMC_12B_Q04.json","sha256":"c9d8107626a54be8b5c7995120ad10ee1d86ab012357c21f86f6cb1723c927da"}],"license":"CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0","licenseURL":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","reviewStatus":"AI-authored and independently AI-reviewed; not teacher reviewed","source":"Original Math Practice solution","sourceRef":"amc/2002_AMC_12B_Q04#bound-integer-sum","sourceURL":"https://github.com/rqzhu-aide/math-practice"}},{"directNodeIds":["cnm_p169","cnm_165"],"id":"bound-and-solve","label":"Bound the integer sum","legacyRouteId":"bound-and-solve","provenance":{"evidence":[{"label":"Legacy graph and grading review","path":"../database/knowledge-graph/evide